# shellcheck shell=sh
#
# Sourced by `uwsm aux prepare-env` (via /usr/libexec/uwsm/prepare-env.sh)
# before the compositor starts. systemd does not read this file and neither does
# your shell; ../install.sh symlinks this directory to ~/.config/uwsm, which is
# where uwsm looks. POSIX sh, not bash -- prepare-env.sh runs /bin/sh.
#
# ~/.local/bin on PATH.
#
# install.sh puts calango-open and apply-gtk-theme there, and two things must
# find them: programs Hyprland spawns directly (a terminal from SUPER+Q), and
# xdg-desktop-portal, which launches the browser handler for every flatpak link.
# The portal reads the systemd user manager's environment, and uwsm fills that
# from this file's result -- before either consumer exists. So one place serves
# both, which is what the block this replaced in hyprland.lua was reaching for.
#
# Why it is not in hyprland.lua any more: `uwsm finalize` exports
# WAYLAND_DISPLAY, DISPLAY and the names in UWSM_FINALIZE_VARNAMES, and PATH is
# not one of them. A PATH set inside the compositor would reach a terminal and
# never reach the portal -- the exact split this file exists to close. (The
# cursor variables hyprland.lua still sets with hl.env ARE covered: the plugin
# at /usr/share/uwsm/plugins/hyprland.sh names XCURSOR_THEME, XCURSOR_SIZE and
# HYPRCURSOR_SIZE among six. Verified present on Fedora 44 too.)
#
# uwsm also sources /etc/profile and ~/.profile, and Debian's ~/.profile already
# adds this directory. Fedora ships no ~/.profile at all, and the ~/.local/bin it
# does add sits in ~/.bashrc, which uwsm never reads -- so on that machine this
# file is the only thing that does the job. The guard below is what lets one
# file serve both: prepend only when missing, so Debian collects no duplicate.
#
# Note the profile is NOT loaded by the `-e` in the session entry's Exec line.
# That flag is desktop_names_exclusive -- "use the names from -D and discard
# other sources". Profile loading is gated on __LOAD_PROFILE__, which
# prepare-env.sh takes from have_env_login: true when `uwsm start` saved a login
# environment, which is what a display manager launching it produces. Read in
# the installed source on both machines, 0.26.4 and 0.26.1.
#
# The colon padding makes the match exact -- without it, a PATH holding
# ~/.local/binx would satisfy the test. The quotes around "$HOME/.local/bin"
# keep it a literal, so a $HOME containing a glob character cannot turn the
# pattern into a wildcard.
case ":$PATH:" in
  *:"$HOME/.local/bin":*) ;;
  *) PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"; export PATH ;;
esac

# Do not point GTK and Qt at an ibus that is not running.
#
# There is no input method on this machine. ../autostart/im-launch.desktop
# switches ibus off and says why -- it segfaulted six times in three weeks, in
# both X11 and Wayland modes, and its only engine duplicated the XKB layout.
#
# So these two must go. GTK_IM_MODULE and QT_IM_MODULE tell each toolkit to load
# an ibus client library and route text input through it. With no ibus daemon
# there is nothing at the other end. GTK falls back quietly, Qt is less
# predictable, and neither has any reason to try. Unset, both toolkits use the
# Wayland text-input protocol their backends already speak.
#
# This block outlived its first purpose. It was written to satisfy ibus's own
# notification, which asks that these two be unset before its Wayland input
# method is configured. That input method is gone; the unset matters more
# without it than with it.
#
# XMODIFIERS and CLUTTER_IM_MODULE stay. Nothing reads them once ibus is absent,
# and leaving them alone keeps this file to the two variables that would
# actually change how a running application behaves.
#
# These are unset rather than never set, because something else sets them first.
# On Debian /etc/profile.d/im-config_wayland.sh sources
# /etc/X11/Xsession.d/70im-config_launch, which exports both. prepare-env.sh
# sources /etc/profile at line 159 and this file later, from load_wm_env -- so
# by the time we run they exist, and the order is what lets us undo them. It
# ends with `exec env -0`, printing the environment it finishes with; a variable
# absent there is never exported to the systemd user manager. Verified on a live
# session: unset here, gone from `systemctl --user show-environment`.
#
# Harmless where the cause is absent. Fedora has no im-config and no
# /etc/profile.d/im-config_wayland.sh, so on epiphany these are already unset
# and `unset` on an unset variable is a no-op that returns 0.
unset GTK_IM_MODULE QT_IM_MODULE
